# `WebsocketProtocol`

<SymbolMeta />
<SymbolSignature />

`WebsocketProtocol` is the server-side seam for a complete WebSocket subprotocol. Implement it in a protocol crate only when that crate owns the raw frame vocabulary and connection lifecycle. Applications normally select an implementation on `#[controller(ws = Protocol)]` and mount it with `AxumAppBuilder::register_ws`.

```rust
use upwell::axum::prelude::*;

struct AcmeProtocol;

impl WebsocketProtocol for AcmeProtocol {
    type Payload = AcmePayload;
    type Outcome = AcmeOutcome;
    type Options = AcmeOptions;
    type BuildError = AcmeBuildError;

    const SUBPROTOCOLS: &'static [&'static str] = &["acme.v1"];
    const REQUIRE_SUBPROTOCOL: bool = true;

    // register, build, and serve define the endpoint lifecycle.
}
```

## Lifecycle

`register` contributes protocol-owned DI components before root-container validation. `build` receives generated `WsControllerDescriptor` values, the application runtime, and one endpoint's options; it returns the endpoint state or a typed startup failure. `serve` owns one upgraded socket: it performs protocol framing, opens message scopes under the supplied connection scope, dispatches to generated message routes, and races socket activity with `WsShutdown` for graceful termination.

`SUBPROTOCOLS` is the server-preference-ordered RFC 6455 token list. Set `REQUIRE_SUBPROTOCOL` when an upgrade without a negotiated token is invalid. HTTP metadata from the upgrade is available through `WebsocketUpgradeMeta`; use protocol state rather than a controller to enforce handshake and serializer rules.

## Controller boundary

The framework stays generic over this trait and does not store a trait object. A `#[controller(ws = P)]` expansion provides a `WebsocketController` implementation and a descriptor whose message routes are built from `#[message]` methods. The protocol decides how an inbound frame selects one of those destinations and how the handler `Outcome` becomes an outbound frame.

`WebsocketProtocol` alone does not imply typed topics. Add `MessagingProtocol` and `PubSubProtocol` only when the protocol supplies a topic body/codec and subscriber-delivery framing.

